{"tool_name":"clair","contents":["biocontainers","oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biocontainers":{"name":"clair","identifiers":["doi:10.1101/865782"],"license":"BSD 3-Clause \"New\" or \"Revised\" License","summary":"Single-molecule sequencing technologies have emerged in recent years and revolutionized structural variant calling, complex genome assembly, and epigenetic mark detection. however, the lack of a highly accurate small variant caller has limited the new technologies from being more widely used. in this study, we present clair, the successor to clairvoyante, a program for fast and accurate germline small variant calling, using single molecule sequencing data. for ont data, clair achieves the best precision, recall and speed as compared to several competing programs, including clairvoyante, longshot and medaka. through studying the missed variants and benchmarking intentionally overfitted models, we found that clair may be approaching the limit of possible accuracy for germline small variant calling using pileup data and deep neural networks.","home_url":"https://github.com/HKU-BAL/Clair","keywords":["Genomics"],"manually_check":true,"total_pulls":9},"biotools":{"id":"Clair","home":"https://github.com/HKU-BAL/Clair","license":"BSD-3-Clause","summary":"Exploring the limit of using a deep neural network on pileup data for germline variant calling.\n\nClair: Exploring the limit of using deep neural network on pileup data for germline variant calling.\n\nClair - Yet another deep neural network based variant caller.\n\nSingle-molecule sequencing technologies have emerged in recent years and revolutionized structural variant calling, complex genome assembly, and epigenetic mark detection","addition_date":"2020-01-14T19:40:37Z","last_update_date":"2020-12-16T13:55:39Z","tool_type":["Library"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:Clair","edit_permission":{"type":"public"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Genome assembly","uri":"http://edamontology.org/op